{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 17, :comments ["<b>And now, let the power of Adonai be great.</b> By way of the plain sense (peshat): one who is slow to anger has great power to break his anger, and this is 'as You spoke, saying' — the attributes; therefore He joined to it 'HaShem, slow to anger.' Or the sense of 'saying' may be at a time of distress, as the Rabbis, blessed be their memory, interpreted the verse (Genesis 28:20), ״וידר יעקב נדר לאמר״ — 'what is לאמר? For the generations,' that they should vow at a time of their distress." "<b>And by way of the Midrash:</b> 'And now let the power of Adonai be great' — let the attribute of mercy prevail over the attribute of judgment." "<b>And know</b> that the letters of the blessed Name — alef, dalet, and yod — are those of יגדל רבתי (גדל). And a parable: to what may the matter be compared? To a groomsman who entered before the king to plead merit on behalf of his daughter. He found the king and the judge occupied with the matter of the daughter. The groomsman began to wonder and said: 'What shall I do? If I supplicate before the king, I need the judge; if I supplicate before the judge, I need the king's honor.' What did he do? He addressed them both together, but mentioned the king first. This is: 'And now let the power of Adonai be great' — immediately the king forgave him and said: ״I have pardoned according to your word״ (Numbers 14:20)." "<b>And by way of the Kabbalah:</b> 'And now let the power…be great' — may the inward power flow and be drawn down into the attributes and not withdraw to the highest of heights. And the explanation of the matter is: when Israel fulfill the Torah and the commandments, the Holy One, blessed be He, rides the heavens, as it says (Deuteronomy 33:26), ״רוכב שמים בעזרך״ — 'who rides the heavens through your help' — that is, through Israel's help He pours power into His attributes, and thereby they add power to the supernal Gevurah, as it says (Psalms 60:14), ״באלהים נעשה חיל״ — 'through Elohim we do valiantly.' And when they do not fulfill the Torah and the commandments, He withdraws from His attributes, as it says (Deuteronomy 33:26), ״ובגאותו שחקים״ — 'and in His pride, the skies' — that is, He exalts Himself, withdraws to the highest of heights, and thereby does not pour His power into the attributes, and thereby they weaken the power above, as it says (Deuteronomy 32:18), ״צור ילדך תשי״ — 'the Rock that begot you, you weaken.' And since Israel were liable to annihilation in this place and were weakening the power above, Moses needed to say now in his prayer: 'And now let the power of Adonai be great,' in order to draw down and pour the inward power into the attributes and that it not withdraw on high — and this is the sense of יגדל, 'be great.' Likewise David, peace be upon him, said (Psalms 40:17), ״ישישו וישמחו בך כל מבקשיך יאמרו תמיד יגדל אלהים אוהבי ישועתך״." "<b>And the Rabbis, blessed be their memory:</b> the masters of truth alluded to the flow and influence by the term 'help,' and this is what the Rabbis, blessed be their memory, expounded in tractate Shabbat: when Moses ascended on high, he found the Holy One, blessed be He, tying crowns to the letters. Moses said to Him: 'Is there no peace in Your city?' He said to him: 'Master of the universe — does a servant give peace to his master?' He said to him: 'Even so, you should have helped Me.' Immediately he said: 'And now let the power of Adonai be great.' And the explanation of the word לעזרני, 'help Me,' is: to draw down to Me power and help." "<b>And know</b> that the fact that he mentioned HaShem, ארך אפים, and did not mention ה' אל רחום וחנון, and did not mention ואמת and נוצר חסד לאלפים, and also did not mention וחטאה — he did not do by chance but with precise providence. And Moses teaches us here the orders of prayer and supplication: how the one who prays should pray and the one who entreats should entreat, so that he may appease his Master. And therefore, of the thirteen attributes that were handed to him at Sinai, he mentioned here only six and omitted seven. He did not mention ה' אל רחום וחנון because they are attributes for one whose merits are the majority, and Israel were now mostly sins. He did not mention ואמת, for by the measure of truth they would be held liable, since it is the measure of justice — as the Rabbis, blessed be their memory, expounded: אמת does not act beyond the strict line of judgment — and they had believed the words of the spies, so that faith was lost and cut off from their mouths. He did not mention נוצר חסד לאלפים because Moses was not now praying in the merit of the fathers, for they were at this moment rejecting the land that the fathers had chosen; and for this reason he did not say ״זכור לאברהם ליצחק״ as he said in the matter of the calf, when he prayed for them in their distress. And he did not mention וחטאה because they were intentional. And he needed to mention in his prayer ה', which is the first Name that was mentioned to him among the thirteen attributes." "<b>And in the tractate</b> Rosh HaShanah (17a): 'here' — before a person sins and repents; 'here' — after he sins and repents. And He has mercy even on one whose sins are the majority, perhaps he will repent, in the manner of what is written (Psalms 145:9): ״טוב ה' לכל ורחמיו על כל מעשיו״. But here he mentioned it only once, which corresponds to 'here' — after he sins — because Israel were here sinning and did not repent. And the explanation of this 'here' — after he sins — is that there is in Him no change or perturbation after the sin as before the sin, unlike a king of flesh and blood." "<b>And you should reflect</b> further on the fact that Moses mentioned it only once — for He is the supreme King from Whom the attributes are drawn forth, and with Him he would speak and from Him request to have mercy upon them, perhaps they will repent; and if they do not repent — You are nonetheless ארך אפים to the righteous and to the wicked; and for this reason he did not say ארך אף. And greater than this: You are רב חסד to one who is half merits and half sins, like one whose merits are the majority, and even without repentance; and if he repents, You are נושא לו עון ופשע — that is, even the intentional sins and the rebellions; and נקה — for those who repent, but He does not clear those who do not repent. And even though He does not clear, He does not annihilate their seed nor cut them off from the world, but He פוקד עון אבות על בנים על שלשים ועל רבעים. And therefore: it is not for You to smite them with pestilence as You said, ״ולהכרית זרעם״, but You can exact and collect Your debt from the children little by little. And the Holy One, blessed be He, answered him: ״סלחתי כדברך״ — as you said concerning them, ״ולא אכריתם מן העולם״, and I shall prolong My patience toward them; and if they have any merit of kindness from the fathers, I will be נוצרו לאלפים; but I do not forgive them the iniquity and the transgression forever, for they shall die in the wilderness and there they shall end — but their children shall come to the land, and I will visit the iniquity upon the children and upon the third and upon the fourth generation, as you said. And from this matter came the decree that was fixed for them: בכיה לדורות, for He visited their iniquity upon their seed. And so Moses in this prayer, since he saw that their sin was exceedingly heavy, did not request in his prayer that He pardon their sin, but only prolongation of patience — that He prolong toward them, as he said: ״פוקד עון אבות על בנים״, and not cast them away by their sin immediately. And therefore the Holy One, blessed be He, acknowledged him at once: ״סלחתי כדברך״ — I shall prolong toward them and visit their iniquity upon their seed; but the men who have seen My glory and My signs and have tested Me shall die in the wilderness, and the children shall enter the land. סלחתי כדברך — the Rabbis, blessed be their memory, understood this verse in many places in the Talmud as having been said concerning the matter of the calf: for he stood forty days, thirty, and they were given the second tablets, and Yom HaKippurim was the day of pardon and atonement, when He said to him ״סלחתי כדברך״ for the sin of the calf; but this sin of the spies He does not pardon — but the men who saw My glory and tested Me shall be punished and die in the wilderness, and their children shall inherit the land. And to this the Sages, of blessed memory, intended when they said: concerning the sin of the calf it was said — and so you should say: concerning the sin of the calf it was said, even though it is written in the context of the sin of the spies." "<b>And in the Midrash:</b> ״סלחתי כדברך״ — the Holy One, blessed be He, acknowledged his words, and said: ״כדברך״ — the Egyptians are destined to say ״כדברך״. Happy is the servant whose Master is numerous and acknowledges to him."], :book :numbers, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 14, :notes {3 "The parenthetical note (גדל) appears in the source between 'rabbati' and the parable; the original reads 'גדל רבתי' as two words, though in biblical Hebrew (Numbers 14:17) it is one word; retained as written.", 6 "Citation reads 'מסכת ראש השנה ה״ ה״'; the double ה״ presumably indicates chapter 5, folio side unstated. Reference is to Rosh HaShanah 17a."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:32:45.316831875Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}